@@ -0,0 +1,35 @@
+# 0001 — Plan changes: upgrades are billed now, everything else waits for the period end
+
+Date: 2026-10-29 · Status: accepted
+
+## Context
+
+Mid-cycle plan changes need a policy for when each kind of change takes effect and how money
+moves. The package has no credit balance, no refunds and no payment layer. Invoices are the only
+record of money owed.
+
+## Decision
+
+- **Upgrade vs downgrade is decided by the plans' monthly list price only**, whatever interval
+  the customer is on or moving to. Switching interval on the same plan is neither.
+- **Upgrades take effect immediately** and are billed today with a prorated two-line invoice: a
+  credit for the unused part of the current plan and a charge for the same days on the new plan.
+  Both lines use the prices of the customer's current interval. Each line is
+  `price × remaining days / period days`, computed exactly and rounded half-up to the cent on
+  its own, and the total is the sum of the rounded lines.
+- **Downgrades and interval switches never take effect mid-period.** They are scheduled and
+  applied by the renewal at the period end, which bills the new plan/interval's full price.
+  Nothing is billed, refunded or credited at the request. Unused annual time is never refunded.
+- An upgrade combined with an interval switch is split: upgrade now, interval switch at the
+  period end.
+- During a free trial every change applies immediately and bills nothing. The trial end does
+  not move.
+
+## Consequences
+
+- No credit-balance or refund concept is needed. A future immediate downgrade or refund feature
+  would supersede this ADR.
+- `Subscription` carries a scheduled change (plan and interval) that the renewal consumes. Any
+  persistent store must save it.
+- Later pricing work must keep in mind that classification uses monthly prices while proration
+  uses the current interval's prices.
